course: biology
lesson: 53
title: How animals defend themselves
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: If you cannot run faster than the fox, you need another plan, and animals have hundreds: spines, armour, stinks, poison, tricks, fake eyes, playing dead and standing together.
explain: A hedgehog in the garden is a good example: when the dog sniffs it, a strong muscle pulls its prickly skin round it like a drawstring bag, and the dog gets a nose full of spines instead of a snack.
explain: Some animals hide, but others want to be seen: the bright blue poison dart frog and the black and white skunk wear warning colours that say, loud and clear, do not even try.
explain: Others use tricks: a lizard leaves its wriggling tail behind, an octopus squirts ink and jets away, a peacock butterfly suddenly flashes big scary eyes.
explain: Cartoons show porcupines shooting their quills like arrows and skunks spraying the moment they see anyone; real porcupines cannot shoot quills, and a skunk stamps and warns first because it would rather not waste its spray.
fact: A hedgehog has about 5000 to 7000 spines, which are stiff hollow hairs, and a strong muscle lets it roll into a tight prickly ball.
fact: Porcupines raise and rattle barbed quills and back into an attacker; they cannot shoot their quills, but the quills come off easily and grow back.
fact: A skunk can spray a very smelly liquid up to about 3 metres, but it usually warns first by stamping, hissing and raising its tail.
fact: Wild poison dart frogs have poison in their skin, made from the ants and mites they eat, and their bright colours warn predators, which learn the colours and leave them alone; zoo-raised ones fed on crickets are not poisonous.
fact: A pufferfish gulps water into a stretchy stomach and swells to several times its size; puffing up is stressful, so it only does it when it must.
fact: Many lizards can drop their tail on purpose; it wriggles to distract the attacker, and the new tail grows back with cartilage instead of bones; growing a new one costs the lizard a lot of energy.
fact: Octopuses and squid squirt dark ink and jet away, and some squid make an ink blob shaped like themselves as a decoy.
fact: A honeybee's barbed sting gets stuck in thick skin like ours and tears away, so the bee dies; wasps have smooth stings and can sting again; the hive is full of the bee's sisters, so dying to protect it protects its family.
fact: Some people are dangerously allergic to stings and need help fast.
fact: The three-banded armadillo is the only armadillo that can roll into a complete ball; most others run or dig quickly into the ground to get their soft belly out of reach.
fact: A peacock butterfly suddenly flashes four big eye spots, which startle a bird before it can think, and even hisses; in experiments, birds were much more scared of butterflies whose eye spots were not painted over.
fact: The Virginia opossum may fall over, go stiff, drool and smell bad when terrified; it is more like fainting than acting, and many predators lose interest.
fact: Musk oxen form a ring facing outwards, with calves in the middle, so wolves meet only a wall of horns.
exercise: What does a hedgehog do when a dog sniffs it? | It rolls into a tight prickly ball | It shoots its sharp spines at the dog's nose | It climbs the nearest tree | It plays a loud warning whistle
exercise: What are a hedgehog's spines? | Stiff hollow hairs | Sharp little bones from its skeleton | Thorns it collects from bushes | Teeth growing out of its back
exercise: What does a porcupine at the zoo do when it feels threatened? | Raises and rattles its quills and backs towards the danger | Fires its quills like arrows across the enclosure at whoever is near | Rolls up into a ball of armour | Squirts ink from its tail
exercise: What does a skunk usually do before it sprays? | It stamps its feet, hisses and raises its tail | It sings a high warning song | It runs round the attacker three times, faster and faster | It turns bright red
exercise: Why are poison dart frogs so brightly coloured? | To warn predators that they are poisonous | To hide among the red and blue flowers of the rainforest | To attract flies to eat | Because they are always cold
exercise: Where do wild poison dart frogs get their poison from? | From the ants and mites they eat | From the dirty water they sit in all day | From the hot sun shining on their skin | They are born full of it no matter what they eat
exercise: How does a pufferfish make itself hard to swallow? | It gulps water and swells into a big prickly ball | It grows a hard shell all over its body in a few seconds | It sinks into the sand | It flashes bright lights
exercise: Why does a lizard leave its wriggling tail behind? | The wriggling tail distracts the attacker while the lizard escapes | The tail was old and worn out and needed changing for a new one anyway | It is too heavy to run with | It is a present for the attacker
exercise: How does an octopus escape in the documentary? | It squirts a cloud of dark ink and jets away | It sheds its whole skin and leaves it behind as a decoy | It turns into a pufferfish | It buries itself in a shell
exercise: Why does a honeybee die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting gets stuck and tears away from its body | It is poisoned by our blood, which is too strong for it to drink | It uses up all its energy flying | It is squashed every single time
exercise: How is a wasp's sting different from a honeybee's? | It is smooth, so a wasp can sting again | It is much longer, hollow and made of something like glass | Wasps sting with their mouths | Wasps have no sting at all
exercise: What can a three-banded armadillo do that most other armadillos cannot? | Roll into a complete ball | Spray a smelly liquid at dogs | Climb the nearest tree quickly | Change its colour to match the leaves
exercise: What does a peacock butterfly do when you get close? | Flashes big eye spots and may even hiss | Stings you with the sharp tips of its wings | Turns completely see-through | Bites with tiny teeth
exercise: What does an opossum sometimes do when it is terrified? | Falls over and lies still as if dead | Climbs into a hole and blocks it with leaves | Screams loudly until its mother comes | Grows its fur twice as long in seconds
exercise: How do musk oxen protect their calves from wolves? | They form a ring facing outwards with the calves inside | They dig snow holes and hide the calves deep under the snow | They run away and leave the calves | They carry the calves on their backs
exercise: What should you do if someone has a bad allergic reaction to a sting? | Get an adult and medical help fast | Wait a day and see if it gets better | Put cold mud on it and carry on playing | Sting them back to cancel the poison out
test: in the cartoon the porcupine fires its quills like a machine gun. can they actually? | No, quills just come off easily when touched | Yes, up to 10 metres, and they can aim them | Yes, but only at night | No, porcupines have only soft fur
test: If porcupines cannot throw quills, how do quills end up stuck in a dog's nose? | The dog touched or bit the porcupine and the barbed quills came off | The wind blew them at the dog | The porcupine stabbed it again and again with its long sharp front teeth | The dog rolled in an old nest
test: A zoo frog is the same bright blue as a wild one. Why is it safe while the wild one is poisonous? | It eats crickets and fruit flies, not the wild ants and mites | Zoo glass soaks up the poison | Zookeepers wash all of the poison off its skin every single morning | It is only poisonous in the rain
test: Why would a poisonous animal want to be easy to see instead of hiding? | Predators learn its colours and leave it alone | It wants to make friends with other animals | It is too slow to hide | Bright colours keep it warm in the shade
test: Why should you never try to make a pufferfish puff up just to see it? | Puffing up is stressful and tiring for the fish | It will explode | It will chase you out of the water and bite your toes | It costs the aquarium money
test: Why should you never pick up a lizard by its tail? | It may drop the tail, and growing a new one costs it a lot | Its tail is poisonous to touch and will burn your hand | It will turn into a snake | The tail is where its heart is
test: mate says squid ink's just them pooing in fright. right? | No, it is a defence: a dark cloud to hide behind | Yes, they only squirt it when they're ill or really scared of something | Yes, it's what they had for dinner | No, it's blood from a cut
test: Why do the eye spots on a butterfly work best if they appear suddenly? | A sudden pair of big eyes startles a bird before it can think | Birds can only ever see eyes that are moving or blinking at them | The spots fade if left open | The butterfly runs out of colour
test: Which is true about the opossum playing dead? | It is more like fainting from fear than acting | It chooses to act dead to make the people watching laugh | It can play dead for only one second | It turns bright yellow while it does it
test: What would be the weak point in a ring of musk oxen? | Any gap, or an ox that panics and runs, letting wolves in | The horns, which are soft | The middle, where the strongest stand | There is no weak point at all, the ring is perfect and wolves always give up
test: Why would digging fast be just as good as armour for an armadillo? | It can get its soft belly safely underground before an attacker comes | Digging makes its shell grow thicker and harder every time | Predators are afraid of mud | Armadillos dig to find more armour
test: Why might a bee give its life for the hive? | The hive is full of its sisters, so protecting it protects its family | It does not like the person it stung | Bees always die after a week anyway, so it doesn't lose much at all | It wants to save its own honey for itself
exam: Identify the principal function of the vivid colouration exhibited by wild poison dart frogs. | It serves as a warning of toxicity to would-be predators | It affords camouflage among the blossoms of the tropical forest | It attracts insect prey within reach of the tongue | It regulates the temperature of the skin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the defensive conduct of the porcupine? | The quills are raised and rattled and detach readily upon contact | The quills are propelled through the air towards the assailant at speed | The quills are concealed until the animal is seized | The quills are discharged together with a noxious spray
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sting of the honeybee. | Being barbed, it remains in the victim's skin and the bee perishes | Being smooth, it may be employed repeatedly against the same assailant | It is withdrawn and regrown within the hour | It is delivered by means of the mandibles
exam: Ascertain the formation adopted by a herd of musk oxen upon the approach of wolves. | A ring facing outward with the young enclosed | A single file retreating at speed | A dispersal of individuals at random | A concealment of the herd within snow drifts
